  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1117 PM CDT THU OCT 1 2026\n\nMoisture continues to gradually stream back into the region under\na stiff south wind, with PWAT values having increased 0.7 inches\nin the last 24 hours. This trend will continue into the weekend,\nwith most of the rain activity remaining diurnally enhanced\nthrough Saturday due to a lack of forcing. During this same time,\nthe cold front to our north will continue to drop south, briefly\nstalling on Saturday as well.\n\nUnfortunately, I think it's worth passing along tonight that our\nQPF amounts through the weekend may be overdone. The bulk of the\nrainfall is expected to fall on Sunday, when a shortwave moves in\noff the Gulf, allowing for that widespread rainfall due to the\nenhanced forcing. Just like I mentioned yesterday, this scenario\nentirely depends on where the cold front stalls, and the\norientation that the cold front takes. The last two nights, I\nhave started to see a trend in the cold front stalling farther to\nthe north, then quickly pushing through the region into Monday.\nNow, we would still see some much needed rainfall if this were to\noccur, but total rainfall amounts would likely be closer to half\nan inch, with more scattered pockets of 1-2+ inches under heavier\nrainfall rates.\n\nThankfully, confidence in this particular scenario is low, as the\nbulk consensus remains the current forecast. However, it's still\nworth passing along given this subtle shift I've seen the last\ntwo days, in the event it continues. Otherwise, the cold front is\nstill expected to push through the region on Monday, with\ntemperatures falling back to a seasonable feel for most of next\nweek.\n\n/44/",
